  "query_preview": "..\nToday: With a ridge developing over the region, the Low over \nMontana starts to shift East. Today will be dry, warm for most \nof the Inland Northwest. The exceptions will be Idaho Panhandle \nand Northeast Washington as the Low\u2019s wrap around moisture moves\nacross these areas. Precip chances will be 10-30% for North \nIdaho Panhandle. The 25-75 probability percentiles range from \n0.03-0.10 of an inch. The northern Washington mountains and \nSpokane to Pullman has a less than 10% chance for precip. High \ntemperatures will be in 70s to low 80s. Overnight lows will be \nin the 40s to low 50s.\n\nWednesday through Monday: The upper level flow pattern shifts \nto a west-east pattern and pushing the stalled Low out of the \nMontana area. The Gulf of Alaska will push a shortwave through \nthe region. Shower activity will spread from the Cascades to the\nIdaho Panhandle on Wednesday through Thursday morning. \nInstability with the wave has thunder probabilities at 10-30% \nfor the higher terrain and up to 10% for the lowlands. The 25-75\nprobability range for precip amount is around 0.1-0.3 of an \ninch through Thursday. Friday will start quiet as a small ridge \npattern develops between the exiting wave and incoming Low. By \nthe afternoon, a tightening gradient will bring breezy \nconditions and elevated fire concerns. Winds will be southwest \nsustained in the teens and gusts ranging from 25 to 35 mph. \nEnsembles have come into better agreement on the Low. It will be\nslow moving and a light precip producer with 24 hour amounts \nless than a tenth of an inch. Cooler conditions are expected. \nHighs will be in the 60s to low 70s. Overnight lows will be in \nthe 40s through Saturday. Sunday morning lows are trending into \nthe mid 30s especially for the northern valleys. Frost pockets \nare possible Sunday and Monday morning. /JDC",
